<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Gonja</h1><p class="st-meta">1675 CE – 1899 CE (approx.) · capital Nyanga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A savanna kingdom in northern Ghana founded by Mande horsemen, whose divisional chiefs rotated the paramount title and whose Muslim scholars wrote its chronicle.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Gonja tradition credits its foundation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndewura-jakpa">Ndewura Jakpa</a>, leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mande-peoples">Mande</a> cavalry from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> region, who conquered the lands between the Black and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-volta">White Volta</a> rivers in the mid-17th century. The invaders, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngbanya">Ngbanya</a>, ruled over many older peoples, and Jakpa divided the conquests among his sons, creating the divisions that formed the kingdom.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The paramount chief, the Yagbumwura, was chosen in rotation from the ruling divisions and resided at Nyanga (Yagbum). Society was layered into the ruling estate, Muslim scholars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mande-peoples">Mande</a> origin and commoners. Gonja controlled the kola trade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salaga">Salaga</a> and Kafaba, linking the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> traders. Muslim clerics wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitab-ghanja">Kitab Ghanja</a>, an 18th-century chronicle of the state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a> defeated Gonja in 1751-1752 and took tribute until 1874, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salaga">Salaga</a> revolted. In the 1890s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> divided the area, and most of Gonja became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-territories-of-the-gold-coast">Northern Territories of the Gold Coast</a>. The Yagbumwura still heads the traditional state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1675 CE – 1899 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:93.03%;width:4.46%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1675 CE – 1899 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nyanga</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ndewura Jakpa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam and traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gonja (Guang)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ghana</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gonja</dd></dl></aside></div>
